    {
      "id": 52,
      "type": "Note",
      "pos": [
        -538.4364779653712,
        236.79004761091005
      ],
      "size": [
        542.40625,
        672.265625
      ],
      "flags": {},
      "order": 8,
      "mode": 0,
      "inputs": [],
      "outputs": [],
      "properties": {},
      "widgets_values": [
        "Matrix decomposition: getting the parts back out of a product\n\nThree demonstrations, each one a round trip - build a matrix from known parts,\ndecompose it, and check that what comes back is what went in. The check is the\npoint: a decomposition that does not reconstruct is a decomposition you have\nmisread.\n\nROW 1 - RQDecomp3x3 on a 3x3.  'M = K @ Q' is assembled from a known upper\ntriangular K and a known rotation Q. cv2.RQDecomp3x3 splits it back into R\n(upper triangular, the intrinsics) and Q (orthonormal, the rotation), and\n'Reconstruct R @ Q' multiplies them again - the two summaries must agree\nelement for element. Note the naming trap: OpenCV's R here is the TRIANGULAR\nfactor, not a rotation.\n\nROW 2 - what the three Q's mean.  The same call also returns Qx, Qy and Qz,\nthe per-axis rotations. 'Qx @ Qy @ Qz' composes them and the product times Q\ncomes back as the identity, which is the only honest way to see that they\ncompose in that order and that Q is their product transposed.\n\nROW 3 - the same call on a 3x4 projection.  'P = K [R | t]' is a full camera\nmatrix. RQDecomp3x3 works on its left 3x3 block, so the reconstruction is\ncompared against that block, not against P.\n\nROWS 4-6 - decomposing a HOMOGRAPHY, which is the one with a real ambiguity.\nH = K (R + t n^T / d) K^-1 is built from a known rotation, translation, plane\nnormal and plane distance. cv2.decomposeHomographyMat returns UP TO FOUR\ncandidate (R, t, n) triples - the maths cannot tell them apart from H alone,\nand two of them survive the cheirality (in-front-of-camera) filter. Picking\none needs outside knowledge: here the known plane normal is dotted against\nevery candidate normal and 'Pick the candidate matching the known normal'\ntakes the best. The chosen R times the true R transposed must be the identity,\nand the chosen t (which comes back scaled by 1/d) times d must return the\noriginal translation in millimetres.\n\nWHAT TO READ: every 'Preview as Text' is named after the value it prints, so\nthe recovered numbers can be compared against the 'Truth ...' nodes on the far\nleft. Nothing here is estimated from pixels - the inputs are exact, so any\ndisagreement beyond floating-point noise is a misreading of the API, not a\nmeasurement error."
      ],
      "color": "#432",
      "bgcolor": "#653"
    }
